Here is the prompt, based on that visible portion: Write a self-contained Python
3 script, intended to run under Termux on Android (use a
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python` shebang), that fetches the
public GitHub repositories of a given user and keeps only those whose primary
language is Python.
Requirements: - Begin the file with the Termux-specific shebang line and `from
__future__ import annotations` so type hints like `list[dict]` work on older
Python versions. - Import only the standard library modules `json`, `sys`, and
`datetime` (including `datetime` and `timedelta`), plus the third-party
`requests` package. - Implement a function `get_user_repos(username: str) ->
list[dict]` that pages through the GitHub REST API endpoint
`https://api.github.com/users/{username}/repos` using `requests.get` with query
parameters `page` (starting at 1), `per_page` set to 100, and `sort` set to
`updated`. - For each page, call `raise_for_status()` on the response and parse
the JSON body.
Stop paginating when a page returns an empty list, or when a page contains fewer
than 100 items. - For each repository on a page, include it in the results only
if its `language` field equals `"Python"`.
For each included repository, build a dictionary with these keys and values
taken from the API response: `name`, `full_name`, `description` (defaulting to
an empty string when missing), `url` (from `html_url`), `stars` (from
`stargazers_count`), `forks` (from `forks_count`), `language`, `created_at`,
`updated_at`, and `private`. - Wrap the request and parsing logic in a `try`
block that catches `requests.exceptions.RequestException` (and any other
requests-related errors as appropriate), reporting the failure rather than
crashing silently.
The exact handling of the error branch should be completed by the agent in a
sensible way: print a clear error message to stderr, and return whatever
repositories were collected or exit with a non-zero status. - Read the target
username from the command line arguments via `sys.argv`, and print a usage
message and exit with a non-zero status if it is missing. - Output the collected
repositories as pretty-printed JSON to standard output using `json.dumps`, with
non-ASCII characters preserved. - Optionally use `datetime` and `timedelta` to
format or filter timestamps if helpful, but the core behavior is listing Python
repositories. - Handle edge cases: users with no repositories, users with no
Python repositories (output an empty JSON list), repositories with a null
description, and private repositories appearing in the response. - Add concise
comments and docstrings consistent with a small utility script, and keep the
code idiomatic and readable. - Make the script executable as a standalone
command with `python script.py <username>`, and include a `main` guard so it can
also be imported without side effects. ---
